1
00:00:31,336 --> 00:00:33,327
﻿Plasma Youth Sports Games
with Unsuccess of the champion,

2
00:00:33,421 --> 00:00:35,860
would like to thank Slaven Bilić
for stepping in as the host.

3
00:00:36,012 --> 00:00:38,974
Bilić is one of the first sponsors,
ambassador and is our biggest promoter.

4
00:00:39,134 --> 00:00:42,240
He used his life and sports experience
in order to prepare high-quality content

5
00:00:42,561 --> 00:00:45,113
with his guests for all generations.
Thanks, Nane!

6
00:00:48,140 --> 00:00:52,580
It’s our great pleasure, after having
Janica Kostelić on our show,

7
00:00:52,780 --> 00:00:54,940
to welcome another woman.

8
00:00:55,380 --> 00:00:59,980
Ana Ivanović, former tennis player,
former number one

9
00:01:00,420 --> 00:01:03,380
and Roland Garros champion.

10
00:01:03,620 --> 00:01:07,740
Thank you for taking the time
to join us. - Thank you, Slaven.

11
00:01:07,940 --> 00:01:10,460
It's an honor to be here with you today.

12
00:01:10,700 --> 00:01:15,780
I haven't had many opportunities to speak,
so it's a pleasure to share this day.

13
00:01:17,580 --> 00:01:19,940
Yes, and I'm sure
it's going to be interesting.

14
00:01:20,220 --> 00:01:25,500
To be precise, you're the one hosting us,
because we're in Palma de Mallorca.

15
00:01:26,260 --> 00:01:30,049
This is your home now, isn't it?
-At the moment, yes, it is.

16
00:01:30,251 --> 00:01:33,405
My children go to school here,
and this is where I live now.

17
00:01:33,530 --> 00:01:35,900
Actually, I've been here
for the past five or six years.

18
00:01:36,037 --> 00:01:38,317
I bought this house back in 2008
while I was still playing,

19
00:01:38,432 --> 00:01:41,041
but over the past few years,
it has truly become my home.

20
00:01:41,187 --> 00:01:44,463
Wonderful. It's beautiful,
my goodness. -It is.

21
00:01:44,618 --> 00:01:47,158
And as you said, life is much more
peaceful here, isn't it? -It is.

22
00:01:47,278 --> 00:01:49,558
We spend a lot of time outdoors,
which is wonderful.

23
00:01:49,689 --> 00:01:52,221
The weather is beautiful here
all year round.

24
00:01:52,356 --> 00:01:55,235
I have three boys,
so life is very active.

25
00:01:55,391 --> 00:01:59,846
And it's always easier to spend time
outdoors with them, to be together.

26
00:01:59,955 --> 00:02:03,916
The schools here are excellent too.
So everything is great. -Wonderful.

27
00:02:05,740 --> 00:02:09,860
So you've been retired from professional
tennis for ten years now.

28
00:02:10,740 --> 00:02:15,620
You're a successful businesswoman.
You even had your own cosmetics line.

29
00:02:16,060 --> 00:02:18,860
And you also have an IT company.

30
00:02:19,300 --> 00:02:22,700
People often say these days that sport
is also a business, don't they?

31
00:02:23,100 --> 00:02:27,620
Does business give you back
that same adrenaline and excitement?

32
00:02:27,860 --> 00:02:30,820
Do you find the same feeling
you had on the court,

33
00:02:31,100 --> 00:02:34,020
the one athletes often miss
after retiring?

34
00:02:34,300 --> 00:02:38,020
Do you find that same competitive spirit
in the business world as well?

35
00:02:38,220 --> 00:02:43,780
No, I don't think that kind of adrenaline
can ever be recreated.

36
00:02:44,020 --> 00:02:46,860
I don't know what it's like
to become a coach,

37
00:02:47,100 --> 00:02:49,660
or whether that feeling
can be replaced.

38
00:02:49,860 --> 00:02:53,140
But the feeling of playing on center court
and competing in major tournaments,

39
00:02:53,340 --> 00:02:55,820
that kind of adrenaline
simply can't be matched.

40
00:02:56,060 --> 00:02:57,860
That pressure, that... -Yes.

41
00:02:58,140 --> 00:03:05,340
And that's why many, in fact most athletes,
are afraid, in a way, to end their careers.

42
00:03:05,540 --> 00:03:10,380
Because they miss that pressure and
the routine they've had since childhood.

43
00:03:10,620 --> 00:03:15,420
But you've said several times
when you've appeared in the media,

44
00:03:15,700 --> 00:03:18,300
that you left tennis behind
and that you don't miss it.

45
00:03:18,540 --> 00:03:23,060
It wasn't an easy decision, and it wasn't
easy to leave tennis behind.

46
00:03:23,340 --> 00:03:27,420
Because from the age of five,
it was my life, my first love.

47
00:03:27,580 --> 00:03:33,020
Something I truly loved
and something I was good at. -Yes.

48
00:03:33,980 --> 00:03:38,620
But I felt it was the right time for me.
I wanted something different.

49
00:03:40,260 --> 00:03:45,420
I wanted to follow a different path
where I could also be successful.

50
00:03:45,660 --> 00:03:48,100
So I can't say that I don't miss it.

51
00:03:48,340 --> 00:03:51,700
I still follow tennis, and I miss
the major tournaments.

52
00:03:51,852 --> 00:03:55,609
I miss the competition,
I miss that feeling, that adrenaline.

53
00:03:55,751 --> 00:03:58,431
But I don't regret my decision.

54
00:03:58,562 --> 00:04:01,781
I'm very happy with where
I am today. -Yes, wonderful.

55
00:04:02,260 --> 00:04:06,940
You have your work, you're a mother,
and you're also a UNICEF ambassador.

56
00:04:07,980 --> 00:04:12,620
Do you have any free time
for yourself? -Not much.

57
00:04:13,620 --> 00:04:18,700
Right now, fortunately or unfortunately,
I occasionally have a free weekend.

58
00:04:18,940 --> 00:04:24,211
So I use that time for myself, my friends,
and to travel. I love traveling.

59
00:04:24,294 --> 00:04:26,594
The irony is that I've spent my
whole life traveling.

60
00:04:26,694 --> 00:04:28,929
And you still love it?
-I absolutely do.

61
00:04:29,254 --> 00:04:32,156
It's my guilty pleasure.
-Traveling. -Yes...

62
00:04:33,260 --> 00:04:37,660
But I truly enjoy being a mother.
It's something I always wanted.

63
00:04:38,500 --> 00:04:42,540
That's my priority, along with
my work, of course.

64
00:04:42,780 --> 00:04:46,900
Because my whole life has been built
around structure and work.

65
00:04:47,100 --> 00:04:50,580
I enjoy working and I enjoy
having new challenges. -Wonderful.

66
00:04:50,820 --> 00:04:56,593
People often say that some people
get stuck in the past and can't move on.

67
00:04:56,913 --> 00:05:03,593
We've been focused on the present,
but now let's go back to the beginning.

68
00:05:04,617 --> 00:05:08,217
You were born in Belgrade in 1987.

69
00:05:08,519 --> 00:05:12,039
You have your mother, your father,
and a younger brother.

70
00:05:12,740 --> 00:05:15,734
Your family is more academic
than athletic, isn't it?

71
00:05:15,956 --> 00:05:18,436
My father played basketball
and then graduated

72
00:05:18,676 --> 00:05:22,596
from the Faculty of Economics,
and my mother is a lawyer.

73
00:05:22,686 --> 00:05:25,086
Yes, she graduated from law school.

74
00:05:25,263 --> 00:05:28,063
My brother is four years
younger than I am.

75
00:05:28,295 --> 00:05:34,320
And I've always been more of a "tomboy".
I never played with Barbies or dolls.

76
00:05:34,500 --> 00:05:36,300
That never interested me.

77
00:05:36,399 --> 00:05:39,599
I loved coloring, drawing,
and playing with balls.

78
00:05:39,980 --> 00:05:43,180
I even have some old videos
from when I was four years old.

79
00:05:43,302 --> 00:05:47,262
I found some plastic tennis rackets,
used them as guitars,

80
00:05:47,489 --> 00:05:52,969
and I kept hitting a tennis ball
against the wall. -Right... How...

81
00:05:54,500 --> 00:05:59,980
When children are young, they usually
become attached to something...

82
00:06:00,180 --> 00:06:06,700
For example, back in Split, we all wanted
to play for Hajduk or Jugoplastika.

83
00:06:07,630 --> 00:06:10,755
Even in an individual sport,
children usually start

84
00:06:10,895 --> 00:06:13,512
by following a star or a local hero.

85
00:06:13,770 --> 00:06:16,817
But in your day, there weren't many
Serbian female tennis players.

86
00:06:16,901 --> 00:06:18,541
There was Monica Seles...

87
00:06:18,735 --> 00:06:21,700
She grew up in Novi Sad,
then moved to the United States,

88
00:06:21,888 --> 00:06:24,848
and I used to watch her matches.

89
00:06:25,016 --> 00:06:28,262
She was my idol, and I wanted
to be just like her.

90
00:06:28,429 --> 00:06:34,301
And since I've always been very stubborn,
things always had to be my way.

91
00:06:34,447 --> 00:06:37,967
I kept telling my mom and dad,
"I want to play tennis!"

92
00:06:38,184 --> 00:06:42,989
And finally, for my fifth birthday,
my parents gave me a tennis racket.

93
00:06:43,069 --> 00:06:45,989
And that was also the sign that they had
enrolled me in a tennis school.

94
00:06:46,220 --> 00:06:47,887
Is this story true?

95
00:06:47,986 --> 00:06:50,770
I'm asking because people
say all sorts of things.

96
00:06:50,897 --> 00:06:57,372
We heard that when you were five,
you memorized the phone number

97
00:06:57,465 --> 00:06:59,545
from a TV commercial
for a tennis school.

98
00:06:59,652 --> 00:07:01,972
And when your mother
came home from work,

99
00:07:02,022 --> 00:07:04,574
you told her to call that number.

100
00:07:04,672 --> 00:07:06,712
You were only five years old,
and your mother thought

101
00:07:06,819 --> 00:07:09,007
you must have remembered
the number incorrectly.

102
00:07:09,120 --> 00:07:11,211
That's true. -And that's...
-Yes, that's true.

103
00:07:11,373 --> 00:07:14,336
The school was called "May 25th",
and it was advertised

104
00:07:14,473 --> 00:07:18,844
during Monica Seles' matches.
And I memorized the phone number.

105
00:07:18,965 --> 00:07:21,180
I've always had a very good
visual memory,

106
00:07:21,305 --> 00:07:24,827
so I told my mother to call it.
And that's where I started.

107
00:07:24,919 --> 00:07:27,852
I trained there
for the first month, indoors.

108
00:07:28,069 --> 00:07:31,445
I think I even have some
old videos from that period.

109
00:07:31,791 --> 00:07:37,235
Then I moved to the "April 11th" tennis
club, which was closer to the Zemun

110
00:07:37,370 --> 00:07:42,492
neighbourhood where I lived.
-So tennis... -Tennis was everything.

111
00:07:42,897 --> 00:07:48,610
But in well-organized countries,
and in better times than Serbia

112
00:07:48,767 --> 00:07:52,479
had back then, it's a sport
that requires total commitment.

113
00:07:53,140 --> 00:07:55,620
It requires the involvement
of the entire family.

114
00:07:55,860 --> 00:07:57,860
We know that from the stories
of Đoković and Ivanišević,

115
00:07:58,060 --> 00:07:59,780
who were guests on our show.

116
00:07:59,980 --> 00:08:03,300
It's one of those slightly crazy,
all-in situations.

117
00:08:03,540 --> 00:08:07,740
But leaving that aside,
you know, at a club,

118
00:08:07,940 --> 00:08:09,900
everything is provided for you.

119
00:08:10,260 --> 00:08:13,300
In your case, you and your family
had to do everything yourselves.

120
00:08:17,220 --> 00:08:20,860
How do you remember those days,
especially considering that Serbia

121
00:08:21,060 --> 00:08:24,780
was at war, under sanctions,
and later bombed? -That's right.

122
00:08:25,140 --> 00:08:28,500
It couldn't have been easy. You must
have had incredible determination.

123
00:08:28,700 --> 00:08:30,860
No, it wasn't easy at all.

124
00:08:31,100 --> 00:08:33,500
And I think if we'd known
what it would all involve,

125
00:08:33,740 --> 00:08:37,716
no one would have dared to do it.
But everything happened very gradually.

126
00:08:37,870 --> 00:08:40,317
At first, it was just an activity
for a child before starting school.

127
00:08:40,466 --> 00:08:43,091
Before tennis, my mother even tried
to enroll me in folk dancing,

128
00:08:43,230 --> 00:08:45,190
which definitely wasn't for me.

129
00:08:45,343 --> 00:08:49,497
It was simply about keeping a child
occupied before starting school.

130
00:08:49,672 --> 00:08:52,312
Then people started saying
that I was talented.

131
00:08:52,434 --> 00:08:56,013
But even then, no one could imagine
where it would eventually lead us.

132
00:08:56,190 --> 00:08:59,390
And of course, those were
the hardest years for our country.

133
00:08:59,889 --> 00:09:03,200
Sometimes I try to imagine
what it was like for my parents.

134
00:09:03,390 --> 00:09:06,830
During what should have been
the best years of their lives,

135
00:09:06,945 --> 00:09:09,513
they were faced with war.
With uncertainty.

136
00:09:09,574 --> 00:09:11,774
No one knew what would
happen the next day.

137
00:09:11,896 --> 00:09:13,896
And yet, their child
was moving forward,

138
00:09:14,010 --> 00:09:18,010
playing a sport that was very expensive
and required so many sacrifices,

139
00:09:18,163 --> 00:09:20,981
and eventually, international travel.

140
00:09:21,042 --> 00:09:23,176
But they were always
there beside me.

141
00:09:23,325 --> 00:09:26,245
My brother also grew up
at the tennis courts.

142
00:09:26,477 --> 00:09:30,411
He crawled there, he ran there,
and even took his first steps there.

143
00:09:30,549 --> 00:09:33,029
And my parents spent every
weekend with me.

144
00:09:33,198 --> 00:09:37,038
Tournaments for children aged ten to
twelve were always held on weekends. -Yes.

145
00:09:38,820 --> 00:09:43,620
These days, people often talk
about the conditions needed

146
00:09:44,180 --> 00:09:47,271
for an athlete to develop.

147
00:09:49,298 --> 00:09:52,006
Sometimes parents push too hard,

148
00:09:52,210 --> 00:09:54,967
and there can even be
too much pressure.

149
00:09:55,616 --> 00:10:03,376
Your story is another example
that ideal conditions

150
00:10:03,780 --> 00:10:07,860
aren't always necessary.
You don't need to have everything.

151
00:10:08,060 --> 00:10:10,597
Of course, you need rackets
and the basic equipment,

152
00:10:10,775 --> 00:10:13,699
but for everything else, you also need
to dream and work hard.

153
00:10:13,844 --> 00:10:17,644
Is the story about the swimming pool true?
Today it almost sounds unbelievable.

154
00:10:17,773 --> 00:10:20,527
Did you actually train
inside a swimming pool?

155
00:10:20,597 --> 00:10:22,854
Yes, that's true.
-What was down there?

156
00:10:22,938 --> 00:10:24,618
Some kind of surface? -Yes...

157
00:10:24,740 --> 00:10:28,167
But even before that,
if I go back to my beginnings...

158
00:10:28,243 --> 00:10:30,443
The support of the whole family
was incredibly important.

159
00:10:30,536 --> 00:10:33,042
My father played basketball,
and he was very talented.

160
00:10:33,138 --> 00:10:35,458
But he never had the support
of his own father.

161
00:10:35,581 --> 00:10:38,981
He wasn't allowed to pursue sports,
because at that time the important thing

162
00:10:39,078 --> 00:10:41,448
was to study and get a proper job.

163
00:10:41,595 --> 00:10:45,808
So when I started playing sports,
my father said from the very beginning,

164
00:10:45,903 --> 00:10:47,743
"I'll take you to practice,
and I'll support you,

165
00:10:47,800 --> 00:10:49,560
if this is what you want."

166
00:10:49,682 --> 00:10:52,995
And that's how I kept going.

167
00:10:53,226 --> 00:10:55,613
In a way, through me,
he was able to experience

168
00:10:55,748 --> 00:10:58,761
something he himself had
never been allowed to pursue.

169
00:10:58,938 --> 00:11:01,498
I never felt any pressure.
-Only the positive kind.

170
00:11:01,697 --> 00:11:04,137
Support and simply being there.
Someone who sees you,

171
00:11:04,267 --> 00:11:08,027
someone who drives you, someone
who's there for you, no matter what.

172
00:11:08,320 --> 00:11:13,580
And yes, that famous swimming pool
was at the "April 11th" sports center.

173
00:11:14,220 --> 00:11:16,900
Because of the sanctions,
the economic crisis and the bombing,

174
00:11:17,140 --> 00:11:23,820
they could no longer afford to heat the
large indoor Olympic swimming pool.

175
00:11:24,700 --> 00:11:28,435
Tennis was usually played on clay courts
under inflatable domes,

176
00:11:28,520 --> 00:11:32,420
but there were no domes there.
So, what did they decide to do?

177
00:11:32,560 --> 00:11:34,982
They realized it would be
more cost-effective to put

178
00:11:35,089 --> 00:11:37,232
a tennis court inside the swimming pool.

179
00:11:37,341 --> 00:11:41,715
They drained the pool and laid down
an ordinary carpet, nothing more.

180
00:11:41,883 --> 00:11:46,458
At first, they installed one full
tennis court with doubles alleys.

181
00:11:46,642 --> 00:11:50,242
However, there was such enormous demand
and so many children wanted to play

182
00:11:50,426 --> 00:11:53,266
that they eventually decided to fit in
two courts without doubles alleys.

183
00:11:53,403 --> 00:11:55,950
They were placed side by side,
almost diagonally.

184
00:11:56,155 --> 00:12:00,826
Janko Tipsarević grew up
playing there with me as well.

185
00:12:01,031 --> 00:12:03,991
We later joked that this was the reason
my down-the-line forehand was so strong.

186
00:12:04,122 --> 00:12:06,202
Whenever we played points,
we had an unwritten rule

187
00:12:06,325 --> 00:12:08,904
that we could not hit cross-court,
because the point would be over,

188
00:12:09,030 --> 00:12:11,190
the wall of the swimming pool
was right there.

189
00:12:11,446 --> 00:12:15,446
So we were forced to make our
down-the-line shots extremely accurate.

190
00:12:15,592 --> 00:12:17,672
But that is not really a joke, is it?

191
00:12:17,881 --> 00:12:22,904
Do you think the swimming-pool wall,
that physical boundary,

192
00:12:23,300 --> 00:12:27,780
actually helped you develop that shot?
-Absolutely. Without question.

193
00:12:28,035 --> 00:12:33,920
I could not play cross-court, and I knew
my opponent would already be moving

194
00:12:33,981 --> 00:12:36,058
towards the middle or covering the line.

195
00:12:36,122 --> 00:12:39,125
So I wanted my down-the-line shot
to be as good and as precise as possible,

196
00:12:39,177 --> 00:12:41,300
because that was how I could
win the point. -Yes, yes.

197
00:12:41,471 --> 00:12:45,058
It definitely helped me develop
that precision. -That is incredible.

198
00:12:45,287 --> 00:12:50,287
So, when someone says "swimming pool,"
your first association is actually

199
00:12:50,408 --> 00:12:56,103
tennis, not swimming. -Yes. Exactly.
-That is remarkable, isn’t it?

200
00:12:57,780 --> 00:13:01,580
There are two other stories about you
that I find fascinating,

201
00:13:01,940 --> 00:13:05,620
and both are completely true.
The only thing I am uncertain about is

202
00:13:05,900 --> 00:13:08,740
when exactly they happened
and how far apart they were.

203
00:13:08,980 --> 00:13:10,900
I would like you to comment on them,

204
00:13:11,140 --> 00:13:17,540
because on the one hand they reveal
love, empathy and sensitivity,

205
00:13:17,820 --> 00:13:22,260
and on the other, remarkable
maturity, clarity and ambition.

206
00:13:22,540 --> 00:13:27,780
The first story is that,
at a time when the country was using

207
00:13:28,020 --> 00:13:32,620
ration coupons for food,
fuel and other essentials,

208
00:13:32,900 --> 00:13:36,020
you told your parents that you did not
want those coupons to be spent on

209
00:13:36,260 --> 00:13:39,660
your tennis. You wanted them to use them
for something the family needed more.

210
00:13:39,900 --> 00:13:44,940
That is true. It happened during
the bombing, coupons were used for petrol.

211
00:13:46,020 --> 00:13:50,060
The club was not that far from where
we lived, but I still had to go there

212
00:13:50,340 --> 00:13:54,620
two or three times, sometimes twice a day,
sometimes every other day.

213
00:13:54,900 --> 00:13:59,580
So I told my father not to spend those
coupons driving me to practice.

214
00:13:59,820 --> 00:14:04,340
I said we should go by bicycle instead,
because it was too far to walk.

215
00:14:04,540 --> 00:14:08,620
To Zemun. -Yes, Zemun,
from New Belgrade. Exactly.

216
00:14:09,860 --> 00:14:14,980
The first time we set off on our bikes,
I had some kind of BMX, I think.

217
00:14:15,106 --> 00:14:17,502
My father did not have a full-sized
bicycle of his own,

218
00:14:17,572 --> 00:14:20,205
so he rode one of those small Pony bikes.
I do not know if you remember.

219
00:14:20,307 --> 00:14:21,953
Yes, the little Pony bikes.

220
00:14:22,082 --> 00:14:26,763
He had to raise the seat as high as
it would go because he is 1.95 m tall.

221
00:14:27,540 --> 00:14:29,940
The sight of him riding that tiny bicycle
was already quite funny.

222
00:14:30,220 --> 00:14:34,740
And because he wanted to help me,
he was carrying my tennis bag as well.

223
00:14:35,180 --> 00:14:40,900
At one point, we came to a street
with tram tracks running across it.

224
00:14:41,068 --> 00:14:44,278
Just as a car was approaching,
my father turned around to check

225
00:14:44,451 --> 00:14:47,529
if I was still behind him and whether
everything was all right.

226
00:14:47,647 --> 00:14:50,935
As he turned, my bag slipped.
He tried to pull it back into place,

227
00:14:51,057 --> 00:14:55,060
turned the handlebars, and the front wheel
became caught in the tracks.

228
00:14:55,173 --> 00:14:57,450
He simply flew over the handlebars.

229
00:14:57,551 --> 00:15:00,021
He was badly scraped and bleeding
all over when he got up.

230
00:15:00,150 --> 00:15:02,708
Then he said, "We are
never doing this again."

231
00:15:03,735 --> 00:15:07,279
Yes. So that is certainly one story we
will never forget. -So it really is true.

232
00:15:07,580 --> 00:15:14,140
Now, there is another story from
that same period of your childhood.

233
00:15:14,700 --> 00:15:17,860
You were already training at the club,

234
00:15:18,100 --> 00:15:20,340
attending group lessons
at the tennis school,

235
00:15:20,620 --> 00:15:24,500
when one day you went to your parents
and said, "Look, this cannot continue.

236
00:15:24,860 --> 00:15:29,460
If I am going to improve,
I need individual coaching.

237
00:15:29,620 --> 00:15:31,740
If there is no money for it,
that is fine,

238
00:15:32,100 --> 00:15:34,260
but carrying on like this
simply makes no sense."

239
00:15:34,351 --> 00:15:36,828
On the surface, that may sound
slightly contradictory,

240
00:15:36,942 --> 00:15:39,742
although it really is not.
A child wants everything,

241
00:15:40,020 --> 00:15:45,060
but those were very mature questions
and decisions for someone so young.

242
00:15:45,300 --> 00:15:48,474
Yes. Honestly, that is one of the more
emotional moments for me because

243
00:15:48,555 --> 00:15:51,475
I remember it so clearly.
I must have been 10 or 11 years old.

244
00:15:51,621 --> 00:15:55,781
I knew times were difficult,
and I was only training in a group.

245
00:15:56,089 --> 00:15:58,998
But there were other children
who were having individual lessons,

246
00:15:59,138 --> 00:16:03,058
and I could see that they were improving
more quickly and playing better.

247
00:16:03,243 --> 00:16:07,203
By then, I was already beating
the other children in my group.

248
00:16:07,325 --> 00:16:10,925
I wanted to move up, practice with the
older players and continue progressing.

249
00:16:11,062 --> 00:16:13,880
I had already asked my mother and father
several times if I could start taking

250
00:16:13,947 --> 00:16:16,667
individual lessons. They would say,
"Ana, you know how expensive that is.

251
00:16:16,749 --> 00:16:18,651
We simply cannot afford it," and so on.

252
00:16:18,718 --> 00:16:21,643
But there was one particular day
I will never forget.

253
00:16:21,733 --> 00:16:25,651
My mother was preparing lunch or dinner,
and I walked into the kitchen and said,

254
00:16:25,800 --> 00:16:33,157
"Mom, I need to tell you something.
I want to train individually,

255
00:16:34,079 --> 00:16:37,156
but if you believe there is no money
for it, then it would be better for me

256
00:16:37,250 --> 00:16:39,564
to stop altogether, because otherwise
you are wasting your money.

257
00:16:39,628 --> 00:16:41,797
If I continue training in a group,
you will keep paying,

258
00:16:41,876 --> 00:16:43,344
but I will not make any progress.

259
00:16:43,442 --> 00:16:46,149
So, there is no point or purpose
in continuing that way."

260
00:16:46,479 --> 00:16:48,634
I remember my mother
stroking my hair and saying,

261
00:16:48,688 --> 00:16:50,689
"All right, sweetheart.
We will see what we can do."

262
00:16:50,983 --> 00:16:54,079
And the following week,
I began taking individual lessons.

263
00:16:54,540 --> 00:17:00,260
That's remarkable. Was that when your
parents decided to sell the house

264
00:17:00,540 --> 00:17:03,780
and move into a smaller apartment so
they could continue supporting you?

265
00:17:04,020 --> 00:17:07,180
That happened a little later. -Later...
-That happened a little later.

266
00:17:07,326 --> 00:17:10,726
By then, though, I had already started
playing in some bigger tournaments.

267
00:17:10,895 --> 00:17:13,415
I was fortunate,
because when I was 12,

268
00:17:13,584 --> 00:17:16,504
something else happened,
another very interesting story.

269
00:17:16,688 --> 00:17:19,368
All of this unfolded within
the space of a year or two. -Yes, yes.

270
00:17:19,532 --> 00:17:22,153
There is a tournament called
Les Petits As, which is probably

271
00:17:22,265 --> 00:17:25,153
the most prestigious tournament in the
world for players under 12. -Right.

272
00:17:25,431 --> 00:17:30,192
We travelled there as a Serbian team.
There was me and another girl,

273
00:17:30,299 --> 00:17:32,779
perhaps two girls,
and two or three boys.

274
00:17:32,909 --> 00:17:36,130
I can’t remember whether
Nole was with us that year.

275
00:17:36,224 --> 00:17:39,151
My parents told me later that they had
already made a decision before I left.

276
00:17:39,240 --> 00:17:40,789
They did not want to tell me at the time,

277
00:17:40,846 --> 00:17:42,706
but that was going to be
my final tournament.

278
00:17:42,753 --> 00:17:45,308
Once I returned home, they were going to
explain that they had run out of money

279
00:17:45,441 --> 00:17:47,441
and that I would have to stop playing.

280
00:17:47,767 --> 00:17:51,017
I went there and lost
in the first round of the singles,

281
00:17:51,140 --> 00:17:53,267
although I think I reached
the doubles final.

282
00:17:53,380 --> 00:17:56,420
The other Serbian girl was ranked
number one in Serbia at the time,

283
00:17:56,620 --> 00:18:00,620
while I was number two, and she was
playing considerably better than I was.

284
00:18:00,860 --> 00:18:04,900
Then one day, while all of us children
were sitting together,

285
00:18:05,062 --> 00:18:08,622
our coach came over and said,
"A sponsor came here today.

286
00:18:08,748 --> 00:18:11,180
He says there is a young girl
he particularly likes,

287
00:18:11,380 --> 00:18:14,380
and he wants to speak to me tomorrow
and see what might come of it."

288
00:18:14,440 --> 00:18:16,381
I simply carried on eating.
It never occurred to me that

289
00:18:16,518 --> 00:18:19,178
he might be talking about me, because
the other girl was playing better.

290
00:18:19,319 --> 00:18:23,399
Yes, of course. -But it turned out
that I was the girl he had asked about.

291
00:18:23,806 --> 00:18:29,233
They invited me to a selection camp.
There were five girls there,

292
00:18:29,340 --> 00:18:33,420
and they were going to choose
two of us to sponsor.

293
00:18:34,722 --> 00:18:36,442
That is how it happened.

294
00:18:36,660 --> 00:18:41,060
They invited me, and a few months later
I travelled to Switzerland on my own.

295
00:18:41,121 --> 00:18:44,444
We spent two weeks undergoing all kinds
of assessments, physical tests,

296
00:18:44,576 --> 00:18:47,381
tennis tests, everything. In the end,
they chose me and one other girl,

297
00:18:47,517 --> 00:18:49,837
who I believe was Hungarian.

298
00:18:50,661 --> 00:18:54,592
They began sponsoring me, and that was
how we got through that period.

299
00:18:54,743 --> 00:18:57,623
I understand. You were 12
when that sponsor came along,

300
00:18:57,729 --> 00:19:00,029
and then the company went bankrupt
about a year later, is that right?

301
00:19:00,165 --> 00:19:03,285
That’s right. -So once again,
it became difficult to keep going.

302
00:19:03,384 --> 00:19:10,104
Exactly. They sponsored me for about a
year and a half before going bankrupt.

303
00:19:10,700 --> 00:19:12,700
By then, I had begun playing
junior tournaments

304
00:19:12,900 --> 00:19:15,660
and was already achieving some results.

305
00:19:15,893 --> 00:19:21,373
Then a friend of my coach Vraneš,
whom I trained with in Belgrade,

306
00:19:22,020 --> 00:19:24,580
went to Switzerland to work as a coach.

307
00:19:24,866 --> 00:19:30,989
There, he met a man who invested in
athletes from various sports,

308
00:19:31,379 --> 00:19:33,374
skiers, Formula 1 drivers and others,

309
00:19:33,501 --> 00:19:35,931
and he was interested in becoming
involved in tennis as well.

310
00:19:35,991 --> 00:19:38,259
He said, "Why doesn't Ana
come over  so I can see her play?"

311
00:19:38,433 --> 00:19:41,713
So, when I was around 14 and a half or
15, my mother and I packed our things

312
00:19:41,889 --> 00:19:43,913
and travelled to Switzerland.

313
00:19:44,017 --> 00:19:46,693
That man went on to become
my manager for my entire career.

314
00:19:47,260 --> 00:19:52,220
And during those crucial years,
when everything begins to change,

315
00:19:52,460 --> 00:19:55,500
during that extremely difficult
transition from junior tennis

316
00:19:55,780 --> 00:20:01,620
into the big leagues, so to speak,
where did you find support?

317
00:20:03,260 --> 00:20:05,860
As you were preparing
to enter the big time,

318
00:20:06,100 --> 00:20:09,860
did you receive the kind of
reassurance a child needs?

319
00:20:09,990 --> 00:20:12,270
Did you know that you were good?

320
00:20:12,457 --> 00:20:15,851
Did you feel that you were better
than the other girls your age?

321
00:20:16,100 --> 00:20:20,540
Coaches often told me that I was
talented and that I had potential.

322
00:20:20,751 --> 00:20:22,911
I had always had a strong forehand,

323
00:20:23,086 --> 00:20:25,726
and that gave me
a certain sense of security.

324
00:20:25,852 --> 00:20:28,812
But during that period,
I was fighting a different battle.

325
00:20:28,903 --> 00:20:30,503
I enjoyed tennis.

326
00:20:30,660 --> 00:20:33,300
I was not particularly concerned with
all the other things surrounding it.

327
00:20:33,403 --> 00:20:36,923
Of course, I dreamed of becoming world
number one, achieving great results

328
00:20:37,046 --> 00:20:39,535
and winning major tournaments,
but above all, I simply enjoyed playing.

329
00:20:39,803 --> 00:20:43,643
The greatest difficulty for me during
that transition was the fact that

330
00:20:43,733 --> 00:20:48,451
I was introverted and very reserved.
I used to cry before every interview.

331
00:20:48,668 --> 00:20:51,232
I would go to the tournament director

332
00:20:51,444 --> 00:20:55,545
to beg them to let me play
on courts that weren't central.

333
00:20:55,785 --> 00:20:59,865
Somewhere out there, court two or three.
So there wouldn't be people watching me.

334
00:21:00,074 --> 00:21:03,874
I had to overcome that.
-Oh, my goodness.

335
00:21:04,580 --> 00:21:09,260
Did you have your own Jelena Genčić,
like Novak did, or not? -No, I didn't.

336
00:21:09,500 --> 00:21:12,340
For me, it was Dejan Vraneš.
He was there during those years,

337
00:21:12,580 --> 00:21:16,780
the most sensitive ones, so to speak,
when it came to technique and tactics.

338
00:21:18,620 --> 00:21:26,300
When you transition... All careers last
a long time, one way or another.

339
00:21:26,740 --> 00:21:31,180
However, a few crucial moments,
especially during those transitions

340
00:21:31,420 --> 00:21:36,180
from adolescence to the senior tour,
is where things are decided.

341
00:21:36,380 --> 00:21:39,500
There, some strong characters,
even if they fall, if they stumble,

342
00:21:39,660 --> 00:21:41,460
they get up and find a second chance.

343
00:21:41,660 --> 00:21:46,380
Those who are a bit weaker or aren't
as talented, they get stuck and vanish.

344
00:21:46,580 --> 00:21:53,268
Was going to those two tournaments
in Japan the turning point where you got

345
00:21:54,136 --> 00:21:58,463
confirmation and everything else? -Yes.
I remember those tournaments.

346
00:21:58,620 --> 00:22:02,060
My mom and I went alone.
I won both tournaments from qualifiers.

347
00:22:02,260 --> 00:22:05,380
That's where I got some
ranking and confidence,

348
00:22:05,580 --> 00:22:08,420
because at one tournament I beat
a girl who at that moment

349
00:22:08,660 --> 00:22:13,060
was ranked 50th or 60th.
I think 6-0, 6-0.

350
00:22:13,980 --> 00:22:18,700
I listened when Novak
was talking to you. -Yes.

351
00:22:18,980 --> 00:22:23,300
That's something where I realized
how much I lacked that in my own career.

352
00:22:23,500 --> 00:22:27,700
When you say mentor, somehow I
had the greatest support from my family.

353
00:22:27,900 --> 00:22:31,740
I was always surrounded
by that love and support.

354
00:22:32,060 --> 00:22:35,460
But in those moments when
you are just coming from the juniors,

355
00:22:35,700 --> 00:22:40,180
and transitioning to the seniors.
Everything is so much different.

356
00:22:40,380 --> 00:22:44,260
Sometimes you lose. I didn't
know how to handle defeat.

357
00:22:44,620 --> 00:22:50,300
Very early on, I had very good results.
In those moments, I didn't have anyone

358
00:22:50,780 --> 00:22:53,860
to support me and to push me,
and to tell me that it was normal

359
00:22:54,020 --> 00:22:57,340
or that it wasn't normal.
Now when I look back,

360
00:22:57,540 --> 00:22:59,500
that is something I would
perhaps change.

361
00:22:59,700 --> 00:23:03,900
To have better support from a team
around me. -I understand, yes.

362
00:23:04,100 --> 00:23:11,620
And now teams are bigger. So, in 2004
you played the final of junior Wimbledon.

363
00:23:14,980 --> 00:23:22,060
From that, to the next... Already in
2005 you won in Canberra... -Yes.

364
00:23:22,220 --> 00:23:27,420
...your first tournament.
Then you beat Martina Hingis in Montreal.

365
00:23:27,660 --> 00:23:33,780
That first, let's say, senior year
you finished as 16th in the world.

366
00:23:34,020 --> 00:23:38,180
You're already recognized.
Talented, one of the most talented.

367
00:23:38,900 --> 00:23:46,060
Greater media attention also begins,
which, as you said, does not suit you.

368
00:23:46,260 --> 00:23:49,180
You run away from it.
Money is also being made.

369
00:23:49,340 --> 00:23:55,060
What did you spend your first cash on?
Do you remember? -I don't remember at all,

370
00:23:55,260 --> 00:23:59,100
because that was never my primary goal.
But I do remember one thing,

371
00:23:59,340 --> 00:24:08,540
the first big gift to myself.
New Year's 2007-2008,

372
00:24:08,780 --> 00:24:15,380
in Hong Kong I bought myself
a Chanel watch that had diamonds.

373
00:24:19,060 --> 00:24:24,700
That year, it was stolen from me in Paris.
When that watch was stolen,

374
00:24:24,900 --> 00:24:29,580
I signed a deal with Rolex,
so it all balanced out. I was sorry.

375
00:24:29,820 --> 00:24:36,540
That was the first thing I wanted and
that I bought for myself. -Bravo.

376
00:24:37,220 --> 00:24:44,500
From that... Now you're a tennis player.
You are, as they say, a shooting star.

377
00:24:45,540 --> 00:24:52,780
From that junior final in 2004
to the first big Grand Slam final,

378
00:24:53,020 --> 00:25:01,900
three years passed. In 2007 in Paris
you lost to Justine Henin, right?

379
00:25:02,100 --> 00:25:07,220
After a streak of 12 matches
that you won.

380
00:25:07,420 --> 00:25:15,100
Back then, you looked invincible.
Defeat... Speaking of defeat.

381
00:25:15,380 --> 00:25:19,540
How did you cope with them? I read
that you sat alone for four hours

382
00:25:19,740 --> 00:25:24,780
in the locker room. -Yes. Defeats
were the end of the world for me.

383
00:25:25,020 --> 00:25:28,620
For four years, I was
the only grandchild.

384
00:25:28,820 --> 00:25:32,980
All the games I played with
my grandmother, I always won.

385
00:25:33,260 --> 00:25:37,860
Later on, I had good results.
I mostly won tournaments.

386
00:25:38,020 --> 00:25:43,420
When I cried for four hours in the locker
room, that was with that manager

387
00:25:43,660 --> 00:25:46,820
from Switzerland, who later was
my manager throughout my career.

388
00:25:47,020 --> 00:25:50,500
When he wanted to sign me,
he came to a junior tournament.

389
00:25:50,740 --> 00:25:54,900
I think it was Bergamo, in Milan.
I lost to some girl

390
00:25:55,100 --> 00:25:59,180
whom I really should have beaten.
I thought it was the end of the world.

391
00:25:59,380 --> 00:26:03,700
He's going to give up on sponsoring me,
and then I have nowhere to go.

392
00:26:03,940 --> 00:26:06,900
I was too afraid to leave
the locker room. I spent four hours

393
00:26:07,100 --> 00:26:10,100
locked inside
the locker room, crying.

394
00:26:10,700 --> 00:26:13,820
Until my mom knocked and said
that he had gone to the hotel.

395
00:26:14,220 --> 00:26:17,140
Then I came out.
-Then you came out? -Yes.

396
00:26:17,380 --> 00:26:21,980
That's a huge responsibility at that moment,
what you feel. And that's the reason.

397
00:26:23,260 --> 00:26:30,860
After that defeat, there's another
defeat in Australia. Among the big ones.

398
00:26:31,660 --> 00:26:36,420
To Sharapova. But that's the first time
that you beat Venus Williams.

399
00:26:36,534 --> 00:26:38,774
Yes. -That's connected.

400
00:26:39,240 --> 00:26:45,820
Then comes this tournament
of your life, right?

401
00:26:46,860 --> 00:26:54,260
In Paris, where you beat Safina.
Not only do you win

402
00:26:54,460 --> 00:26:58,500
Roland Garros, a Grand Slam.
You become number one,

403
00:26:58,740 --> 00:27:05,740
and you're a kid. You're 20 years old.
How do you even remember that tournament?

404
00:27:06,060 --> 00:27:08,620
Somehow, it came naturally.

405
00:27:08,780 --> 00:27:13,180
A year earlier, I was in the final,
and I will never forget that defeat

406
00:27:13,420 --> 00:27:19,460
by Justine. Back then, I "froze."
I played well the whole tournament.

407
00:27:19,700 --> 00:27:23,740
In 2007, in the semifinals,
I beat Sharapova, I think 6-0, 6-1.

408
00:27:23,980 --> 00:27:28,740
I get to the final against Justine,
first game, I break. I lead 40-0

409
00:27:28,940 --> 00:27:33,300
and suddenly realize where I am,
what is happening. I remember that I

410
00:27:33,500 --> 00:27:37,020
couldn't toss the ball up anymore.
I couldn't move,

411
00:27:37,220 --> 00:27:43,140
I couldn't feel my legs. I lost 6-2, 6-1.
Then came that Australian Open,

412
00:27:43,340 --> 00:27:47,700
when I lost in the final to Sharapova.
It was one of the tougher defeats

413
00:27:47,900 --> 00:27:53,580
for me because I felt ready.
I won't forget, but I literally lost

414
00:27:53,740 --> 00:27:58,140
the match because of a single shot.
It was 5-5 in the first set, 30-30.

415
00:27:58,340 --> 00:28:02,940
She was serving, I had a setup
on my forehand. For some reason,

416
00:28:03,140 --> 00:28:06,660
I decided to play a drop shot
from the forehand side,

417
00:28:06,860 --> 00:28:10,620
which is usually not used that often.
I hit the bottom of the net.

418
00:28:10,860 --> 00:28:15,100
I lose the point, I lose the game,
7-5, 6-2. I couldn't recover...

419
00:28:15,300 --> 00:28:18,900
You can't bounce back from that?
-Yes. And that's...

420
00:28:19,100 --> 00:28:24,620
When you enter that kind of spiral,
sometimes it was so hard to get out of it.

421
00:28:25,420 --> 00:28:29,140
It didn't matter anymore
what she was going to do.

422
00:28:29,380 --> 00:28:34,580
It was more about my feelings,
my game plan, tactics, and all that.

423
00:28:34,740 --> 00:28:37,580
Everything literally vanished.
I was only thinking about that shot

424
00:28:37,740 --> 00:28:42,980
and why I chose that shot.
Then, somehow, that Roland Garros 2008,

425
00:28:43,260 --> 00:28:48,340
a lot of things had already "clicked" for me.
I already had a different kind of experience.

426
00:28:48,700 --> 00:28:52,820
That tournament wasn't easy at all.
Throughout the whole tournament I had

427
00:28:53,020 --> 00:28:57,060
a lot of three-set matches.
With Jankovic in the semifinals.

428
00:28:57,780 --> 00:29:00,980
Somehow that final...
I knew that Safina was tired,

429
00:29:01,220 --> 00:29:04,220
she also had a very
hard path to the final.

430
00:29:04,460 --> 00:29:08,180
I used that experience that I had.
I was really focused.

431
00:29:08,380 --> 00:29:14,180
I knew exactly what my goal was,
how I should play.

432
00:29:14,620 --> 00:29:19,300
Afterwards, it was quite emotional,
but my parents were there too. -Yes.

433
00:29:19,580 --> 00:29:25,820
And which match besides the final
was the hardest for you? Was it Jelena?

434
00:29:26,060 --> 00:29:31,700
And that's strange. From Serbia,
two of you in the semifinals.

435
00:29:31,820 --> 00:29:34,260
It's not America. -Yes, yes.

436
00:29:34,620 --> 00:29:40,020
At that moment, tennis in Serbia
is booming. Both men's and women's.

437
00:29:40,300 --> 00:29:44,860
I think the match with Jelena was harder
for me than the final with Safina.

438
00:29:45,140 --> 00:29:48,100
Another match was also very
hard for me that year.

439
00:29:48,300 --> 00:29:54,620
I think it was the fourth round,
against the Spaniard, Medina Garrigues.

440
00:29:55,220 --> 00:29:58,740
I need to double-check,
but I know I played...

441
00:29:58,940 --> 00:30:02,460
I don't know if it was that year or a year
earlier, but I think it was that year.

442
00:30:02,700 --> 00:30:08,225
We played three sets, I barely
won on clay, that was really grueling.

443
00:30:09,940 --> 00:30:14,180
So, you are 20 years old,
you win Roland Garros.

444
00:30:14,420 --> 00:30:17,540
Do you remember the reception
in Belgrade? -Of course.

445
00:30:17,740 --> 00:30:23,060
Probably that is... I was thinking,
that's probably the moment

446
00:30:23,260 --> 00:30:29,700
when you slowly become aware of what you've
done and what it means to people back home.

447
00:30:29,900 --> 00:30:35,300
Especially in a sport that is individual.
In ours, it's different.

448
00:30:35,500 --> 00:30:39,682
Football, basketball, team sports.
You live with those fans.

449
00:30:40,010 --> 00:30:43,500
They cheer for your club,
then criticize you when you lose.

450
00:30:44,180 --> 00:30:49,980
But you feel them. You play over
there somewhere in Paris, you win,

451
00:30:50,180 --> 00:30:55,100
and suddenly half the city
is waiting for you, ecstatic. -Yes.

452
00:30:55,300 --> 00:30:58,860
But then your heart is this big.
-Yes. The only thing I regret

453
00:30:59,060 --> 00:31:03,500
is that I never had a tournament
at home. -Yes.

454
00:31:03,740 --> 00:31:07,380
That I never played in front
of my own fans.

455
00:31:07,620 --> 00:31:11,860
I think that for every athlete
that is a very special feeling.

456
00:31:12,060 --> 00:31:16,740
As you said, that connection was different.
I knew I had great support,

457
00:31:16,940 --> 00:31:21,740
but the interaction wasn't there.
I wish it had been. -Yes.

458
00:31:23,060 --> 00:31:29,460
After that comes a drop.
Comes a drop which is, which is...

459
00:31:30,820 --> 00:31:33,620
Which is a combination
of several things.

460
00:31:33,820 --> 00:31:36,540
Number one, you got
injured right after that,

461
00:31:36,740 --> 00:31:40,940
so because of that you missed
the Olympic Games in Beijing.

462
00:31:41,300 --> 00:31:51,460
You mention a very interesting statement.
You started to feel the pressure

463
00:31:51,700 --> 00:31:59,180
only after you did great things. -Yes.
-You become the hunted, not the hunter.

464
00:32:00,140 --> 00:32:05,380
Do you think something similar
is happening to Emma Raducanu?

465
00:32:05,580 --> 00:32:08,260
It's possible.
I don't know her personally.

466
00:32:08,740 --> 00:32:12,700
But I think every athlete
has their own battles.

467
00:32:12,900 --> 00:32:15,860
In an individual sport,
it's a little different because,

468
00:32:16,460 --> 00:32:19,740
as teenagers, we're already competing
in front of millions of people.

469
00:32:19,980 --> 00:32:25,800
There's nowhere to hide, no chance
to reset, and no time to do it.

470
00:32:25,906 --> 00:32:28,506
The tournaments keep coming,
the ranking points keep adding up,

471
00:32:28,565 --> 00:32:31,165
and if you don't defend them...
-It's a machine, isn't it? -Yes.

472
00:32:32,060 --> 00:32:37,207
I remember that year, after Roland Garros,
I lost in the third round at Wimbledon.

473
00:32:37,803 --> 00:32:40,537
I can't say it was unexpected,
because I was genuinely exhausted,

474
00:32:40,605 --> 00:32:43,488
both physically and emotionally,
and I was carrying

475
00:32:43,606 --> 00:32:44,756
a huge amount of pressure.

476
00:32:44,855 --> 00:32:48,910
There were so many things I had to do.
Everything was new.

477
00:32:49,583 --> 00:32:54,113
After Wimbledon, I had a training block,
and that's when I got injured.

478
00:32:54,422 --> 00:32:57,712
I developed a cyst in my right wrist,
and I couldn't do anything.

479
00:32:57,868 --> 00:33:00,895
I couldn't tie my hair,
I couldn't even brush my teeth.

480
00:33:01,070 --> 00:33:03,004
I literally couldn't use my right hand.

481
00:33:03,173 --> 00:33:06,603
Then, very soon afterward,
it was time for the Beijing Olympics.

482
00:33:06,864 --> 00:33:10,884
I went to Beijing hoping
I'd be able to compete.

483
00:33:11,109 --> 00:33:15,157
That was probably the hardest moment
of my career - being the world No. 1

484
00:33:15,246 --> 00:33:17,876
and having to withdraw
from the Olympic Games,

485
00:33:17,973 --> 00:33:22,133
unable to represent my country.
We compete under the Serbian flag,

486
00:33:22,333 --> 00:33:25,321
but the Olympic Games
are something truly special.

487
00:33:25,740 --> 00:33:28,180
That was incredibly difficult for me.

488
00:33:28,259 --> 00:33:31,228
I even flew from Beijing to Sydney
to see a specialist,

489
00:33:31,439 --> 00:33:34,100
to find out whether there was anything
he could do to help,

490
00:33:34,300 --> 00:33:36,689
whether I needed surgery,
or something else.

491
00:33:36,765 --> 00:33:40,001
That was a couple of weeks later,
just before the US Open.

492
00:33:40,559 --> 00:33:42,839
And then, of course,
once all of that happens,

493
00:33:42,992 --> 00:33:44,912
you inevitably lose some confidence.

494
00:33:45,033 --> 00:33:47,673
And if you don't have
a strong support system,

495
00:33:47,796 --> 00:33:49,461
especially when it comes to coaches...

496
00:33:49,581 --> 00:33:52,587
Back then, hardly anyone
had mental performance

497
00:33:52,729 --> 00:33:56,587
coaches or sports psychologists.
Today, you see that much more often.

498
00:33:56,660 --> 00:33:58,704
Some athletes even travel
with psychologists.

499
00:33:58,793 --> 00:34:00,938
It all turns into a snowball effect.

500
00:34:01,620 --> 00:34:04,700
If it were just one issue,
it would be easy to fix.

501
00:34:05,020 --> 00:34:09,940
But this was a combination
of many different things.

502
00:34:10,300 --> 00:34:13,140
Then you're not competing,
your confidence drops,

503
00:34:13,380 --> 00:34:17,700
and at the same time,
you start to lose focus a little.

504
00:34:17,940 --> 00:34:22,300
Meanwhile, your popularity is growing,
and you're becoming a global star.

505
00:34:22,460 --> 00:34:25,940
You have endorsement deals
and commercial commitments,

506
00:34:26,060 --> 00:34:29,140
and managing all of that isn't easy.

507
00:34:29,340 --> 00:34:34,500
Then came 2009, which you've described
as perhaps the toughest year of your career.

508
00:34:35,500 --> 00:34:38,820
I think there was a stretch
of 17 tournaments

509
00:34:39,020 --> 00:34:41,260
where you didn't reach
a single quarterfinal. -Yes.

510
00:34:41,460 --> 00:34:47,927
I used to think 2009 was my hardest year,
but then 2010 came along.

511
00:34:49,100 --> 00:34:54,740
I remember dropping all the way
to around No. 70 in the rankings.

512
00:34:54,980 --> 00:34:58,687
By the end of the season, I barely made it
into the main draw in Cincinnati.

513
00:35:00,100 --> 00:35:03,500
Because in 2009, I was still getting
relatively good results.

514
00:35:03,638 --> 00:35:08,158
I reached the final at Indian Wells.
But in 2010, not really.

515
00:35:08,740 --> 00:35:13,300
That period was incredibly difficult for me.
I remember my coach leaving me.

516
00:35:13,453 --> 00:35:15,906
He told me he didn't want to work
with me anymore.

517
00:35:16,098 --> 00:35:19,679
I was caught between
different coaches, a new team,

518
00:35:19,791 --> 00:35:22,210
and everything else that was going on.

519
00:35:22,427 --> 00:35:26,007
In Cincinnati, I came to the tournament
ranked somewhere around No. 70.

520
00:35:26,229 --> 00:35:29,445
I remember playing Azarenka
in either the first or second round.

521
00:35:29,594 --> 00:35:31,570
She had two match points,

522
00:35:31,710 --> 00:35:34,670
and I managed to save both of them
and win the match.

523
00:35:34,909 --> 00:35:38,124
After that, I went on
to reach the semifinals.

524
00:35:38,188 --> 00:35:40,570
That immediately moved me
back up the rankings,

525
00:35:40,716 --> 00:35:45,437
and I was able to get into the main draws
of the biggest tournaments again.

526
00:35:45,769 --> 00:35:49,078
Listening to you tell the story now,
it almost sounds straightforward...

527
00:35:49,138 --> 00:35:50,928
But it wasn't. It lasted.

528
00:35:51,044 --> 00:35:53,414
This wasn't something that was
over in a couple of days.

529
00:35:53,618 --> 00:35:57,906
Did you ever think about walking away
from tennis altogether? -Definitely.

530
00:35:58,377 --> 00:36:00,857
I think that thought crosses
every athlete's mind at some point.

531
00:36:01,104 --> 00:36:05,344
I remember it happened to me
during that 2010 season...

532
00:36:06,069 --> 00:36:12,015
I think I was in San Diego.
I was about 22 or 23 years old

533
00:36:12,137 --> 00:36:15,007
when I said to my mom, "If I were 27,
I'd retire  from tennis right now."

534
00:36:15,282 --> 00:36:17,373
But it was a strange feeling
because I kept asking myself,

535
00:36:17,472 --> 00:36:19,506
"What else would I even do?"

536
00:36:20,680 --> 00:36:23,217
When you're constantly in the spotlight,
every week you compete,

537
00:36:23,328 --> 00:36:26,168
you have press conferences,
and people keep asking

538
00:36:26,254 --> 00:36:28,763
what's wrong and why things
aren't working.

539
00:36:28,833 --> 00:36:31,140
If we actually had the answers
to those questions,

540
00:36:31,219 --> 00:36:32,950
we'd solve the problem.

541
00:36:34,140 --> 00:36:38,740
So it's an incredibly emotional experience,
especially for a young woman.

542
00:36:40,700 --> 00:36:43,580
Where did you find the strength
to come back and return

543
00:36:43,860 --> 00:36:46,340
to the Top 5 in 2015?

544
00:36:48,420 --> 00:36:52,740
You keep talking about
psychology and mental strength

545
00:36:52,980 --> 00:36:55,980
as the reason behind
both the highs and the lows.

546
00:36:56,260 --> 00:36:59,297
Back then, though, you didn't have
a sports psychologist on your team.

547
00:36:59,439 --> 00:37:01,799
Did you read psychology books?
-I did.

548
00:37:02,147 --> 00:37:06,735
You sought solution,
how to overcome those things.

549
00:37:07,237 --> 00:37:10,951
I was always fascinated by psychology,
and I actually wanted to study it.

550
00:37:11,079 --> 00:37:13,927
Even today, it's something
that really interests me.

551
00:37:14,169 --> 00:37:17,013
The challenge is that
everything can be viewed

552
00:37:17,213 --> 00:37:19,474
from either a positive
or a negative perspective.

553
00:37:19,634 --> 00:37:23,912
At certain points, I found myself sinking
into some very difficult emotions,

554
00:37:24,618 --> 00:37:27,778
even psychosomatic issues
and all the feelings that came with them.

555
00:37:28,002 --> 00:37:32,255
I wanted to disappear. I wanted to hide
and get away from the spotlight.

556
00:37:32,620 --> 00:37:36,279
Throughout my entire career,
from the very beginning until today,

557
00:37:36,450 --> 00:37:38,521
my family has been
my greatest source of support.

558
00:37:38,698 --> 00:37:42,545
I remember being in Eastbourne,
I don't remember exactly what year it was,

559
00:37:42,747 --> 00:37:47,267
when my brother Miloš said something
to me during one of our conversations.

560
00:37:47,419 --> 00:37:52,341
He said, "Ana, honestly, who cares?
Do it for yourself. Who cares?"

561
00:37:52,497 --> 00:37:55,257
But you're constantly trying
to please someone else.

562
00:37:55,359 --> 00:37:57,966
Your sponsors. Or you're thinking about
what other people expect from you,

563
00:37:58,056 --> 00:38:00,646
what they believe is best for you,
or what everyone is saying.

564
00:38:00,795 --> 00:38:04,123
People think, "She should play this way,"
or, "She should be doing that."

565
00:38:04,466 --> 00:38:06,990
And somewhere along the way,
you lose yourself.

566
00:38:07,267 --> 00:38:11,623
Then he said, "Why do you care?
Just do what you believe is best."

567
00:38:11,774 --> 00:38:14,774
That was a real reset for me.
It was a wake-up call.

568
00:38:14,840 --> 00:38:16,810
And I thought, "You're right."

569
00:38:16,940 --> 00:38:18,626
From then on,
I focused on my own path.

570
00:38:18,748 --> 00:38:20,854
Because, at the end of the day,
what people read,

571
00:38:20,951 --> 00:38:24,870
what they see, what they hear,
that's only the tip of the iceberg, isn't it?

572
00:38:25,042 --> 00:38:26,017
Absolutely.

573
00:38:26,318 --> 00:38:30,479
What really matters is everything beneath
the surface, your feelings, your beliefs,

574
00:38:30,532 --> 00:38:32,372
because every one of us is different.

575
00:38:32,486 --> 00:38:34,651
You can't judge a person
if you don't really know them.

576
00:38:34,835 --> 00:38:37,721
Exactly. -If you only read the cover,
you don't know what's inside the book.

577
00:38:37,896 --> 00:38:40,893
That's true. People are very quick
to judge these days.

578
00:38:41,294 --> 00:38:45,614
And then there's one quote of yours
that really sums all of this up.

579
00:38:45,901 --> 00:38:51,057
It's a remarkable statement,
and it reveals a lot.

580
00:38:51,347 --> 00:38:54,166
You once said that, in a way,
winning Roland Garros

581
00:38:54,380 --> 00:38:56,540
probably came a few years too early.

582
00:38:56,772 --> 00:39:00,012
That reminds me of something
Becker once said.

583
00:39:00,497 --> 00:39:03,457
He said that if he'd known
what his life would be like

584
00:39:03,579 --> 00:39:05,899
after winning Wimbledon at 17,
he would have missed

585
00:39:06,091 --> 00:39:09,451
that final volley on purpose.

586
00:39:10,116 --> 00:39:13,536
He won a few more Wimbledons,
so there's not much to complain about.

587
00:39:13,781 --> 00:39:17,224
And you may not have won Wimbledon,
but you achieved just about everything else.

588
00:39:17,465 --> 00:39:19,966
You retired in 2016, right?

589
00:39:20,097 --> 00:39:23,528
Was that a decision you'd been
thinking about for a long time,

590
00:39:23,792 --> 00:39:28,880
or was it more of a sudden moment,
that's it, I'm done?

591
00:39:29,168 --> 00:39:31,968
No. It had been brewing
in my mind for a long time.

592
00:39:32,146 --> 00:39:35,026
I remember saying, even when
I was still in my twenties,

593
00:39:35,164 --> 00:39:37,804
"I'm not someone
who's going to play until I'm 40.

594
00:39:38,060 --> 00:39:39,900
That never appealed to me."

595
00:39:40,140 --> 00:39:42,980
I always knew I wanted
other things in life,

596
00:39:43,700 --> 00:39:47,700
and that one day I wanted
a different kind of career as well.

597
00:39:48,900 --> 00:39:53,860
I wanted to give something back,
to pass on what I'd received from tennis

598
00:39:54,580 --> 00:39:57,180
and from all the opportunities
tennis had given me.

599
00:39:57,420 --> 00:40:00,180
But I remember that period very clearly.

600
00:40:00,380 --> 00:40:04,140
In 2014, I fought my way back
into the Top 5.

601
00:40:04,580 --> 00:40:10,660
I played tournament in Singapore.
Then I started 2015 really well.

602
00:40:10,806 --> 00:40:13,966
I reached the final in Brisbane and
lost to Sharapova in three sets,

603
00:40:14,112 --> 00:40:15,912
even though I'd been ahead in the match.

604
00:40:16,113 --> 00:40:18,593
Then I went to the Australian Open,
and one morning,

605
00:40:18,754 --> 00:40:22,194
two or three days before the tournament
tarted, I was getting out of the shower,

606
00:40:22,379 --> 00:40:27,219
hit my foot against the door,
and broke my little toe.

607
00:40:27,506 --> 00:40:30,954
Nobody knew about it because
I didn't really talk about it publicly.

608
00:40:31,107 --> 00:40:33,735
The problem was,
there wasn't much they could do.

609
00:40:33,883 --> 00:40:36,336
You couldn't put it in a cast
or anything. -Right.

610
00:40:36,727 --> 00:40:41,368
They told me, "Look, we can give you
painkillers, and you can try to play.

611
00:40:41,572 --> 00:40:47,532
Or you can withdraw,
wait for it to heal naturally."

612
00:40:50,180 --> 00:40:55,380
I decided to play with pain medication,
but I just couldn't.

613
00:40:55,911 --> 00:40:58,471
Your mind is constantly
focused on the pain,

614
00:40:58,718 --> 00:41:01,078
and you always feel
that something isn't right.

615
00:41:01,246 --> 00:41:04,688
What happened afterward was even worse.
The bone never healed properly,

616
00:41:04,893 --> 00:41:08,613
and I developed constant inflammation
in that part of my foot.

617
00:41:08,753 --> 00:41:12,472
My toe was swollen almost every day.
Besides taking painkillers,

618
00:41:12,698 --> 00:41:16,933
I actually had to cut open my tennis shoes
just so I could get my foot into them.

619
00:41:17,157 --> 00:41:19,824
Every time I tried to put on a shoe,
the pain was unbearable.

620
00:41:20,330 --> 00:41:23,965
Because of that, I changed
the way I moved on the court,

621
00:41:24,216 --> 00:41:27,096
and eventually my knee
started suffering as well.

622
00:41:27,320 --> 00:41:30,440
Then I began feeling pain in my knee
whenever I had to run

623
00:41:30,900 --> 00:41:34,808
for any extended period.
Little by little, all of it added up.

624
00:41:34,917 --> 00:41:39,129
Mentally, I became exhausted
from constantly fighting the inflammation

625
00:41:39,393 --> 00:41:42,379
and relying on pain
medication all the time.

626
00:41:42,586 --> 00:41:45,363
At that point, I was still ranked
around No. 15 in the world.

627
00:41:45,533 --> 00:41:47,910
But I no longer felt that
I had the strength within me

628
00:41:48,043 --> 00:41:50,230
to fight my way back
to the very top again.

629
00:41:50,369 --> 00:41:54,918
And I didn't want to spend years
ranked No. 15, No. 20, or No. 30,

630
00:41:55,021 --> 00:41:57,558
after the kind of career I'd already had.

631
00:41:57,940 --> 00:42:00,700
You've never regretted
that decision? -No.

632
00:42:00,900 --> 00:42:04,460
Even though today we see
so many examples...

633
00:42:04,590 --> 00:42:07,710
Like Serena Williams?
They come back, because they miss it.

634
00:42:07,918 --> 00:42:12,398
It's not, as people say, about the money.
It was never about money.

635
00:42:13,300 --> 00:42:15,940
So "never" really means never?
-No, never.

636
00:42:16,140 --> 00:42:20,420
For the first couple of years, I didn't
even feel like picking up a racket.

637
00:42:20,620 --> 00:42:24,540
I genuinely didn't miss it. I still
love watching tennis to this day.

638
00:42:24,662 --> 00:42:27,182
I recently played in the Legends
event at Roland Garros,

639
00:42:27,366 --> 00:42:31,253
and it was wonderful to be back on
those courts, to be part of it again,

640
00:42:31,434 --> 00:42:36,354
and to see all those people.
But I didn't want to compete.

641
00:42:36,709 --> 00:42:42,612
Maybe that's because, in reality, I spent
so much of my career fighting against it,

642
00:42:42,811 --> 00:42:47,847
fighting against myself, because
I was constantly in the spotlight.

643
00:42:48,018 --> 00:42:52,401
And that part of my life was finally gone.
During the last few years of my career,

644
00:42:52,511 --> 00:42:56,315
I suffered from severe panic attacks
whenever I walked onto the court.

645
00:42:56,432 --> 00:42:58,088
During the last few years?

646
00:42:58,198 --> 00:43:00,113
I'd think by then you would
have gotten used to it.

647
00:43:00,230 --> 00:43:01,979
Isn't that something that usually
happens at the beginning?

648
00:43:02,036 --> 00:43:03,965
I remember being terrified
before my first interview.

649
00:43:04,082 --> 00:43:09,262
By the second, third, or fifth one,
it becomes routine.

650
00:43:09,837 --> 00:43:16,926
I never really talked about it, but
I had very severe panic attacks. -Yes.

651
00:43:17,575 --> 00:43:19,735
And then people would say,
"What's happening?

652
00:43:19,826 --> 00:43:22,306
Why does she suddenly collapse
in the middle of a match?"

653
00:43:22,405 --> 00:43:25,606
But what was really happening was
that the panic would take over...

654
00:43:25,678 --> 00:43:28,232
And I simply couldn't go on
because I was overwhelmed by fear.

655
00:43:28,424 --> 00:43:30,744
Then the next day,
I had to go back out there.

656
00:43:30,915 --> 00:43:33,434
And play in front of a crowd all over
again. It's unbelievable, really.

657
00:43:33,760 --> 00:43:36,400
So you were carrying
a tremendous burden?

658
00:43:36,980 --> 00:43:40,180
That's one of the reasons I didn't miss
tennis that much. -Good for you.

659
00:43:40,500 --> 00:43:47,660
Do you know what's so contradictory?
When I look at your career...

660
00:43:51,220 --> 00:43:55,940
Fifteen titles, a Grand Slam,
world No. 1...

661
00:43:56,016 --> 00:43:59,065
There isn't anything higher than that.
That's the ultimate achievement, isn't it?

662
00:43:59,386 --> 00:44:03,595
For every child, for every young tennis
player starting out,

663
00:44:03,781 --> 00:44:05,596
that's the absolute dream.

664
00:44:05,770 --> 00:44:10,350
And yet, for a 20-year-old who was
already world No. 1

665
00:44:10,555 --> 00:44:11,995
and winning major titles...?

666
00:44:12,980 --> 00:44:15,580
Do you ever think things could
have turned out even better?

667
00:44:15,780 --> 00:44:18,300
I think so.
That's exactly why I struggled.

668
00:44:18,500 --> 00:44:22,700
That's why I went through all of that,
because I knew I was capable of more.

669
00:44:22,900 --> 00:44:25,740
But there was something
stronger than me.

670
00:44:25,940 --> 00:44:29,620
I started playing tennis simply
because I loved the sport.

671
00:44:29,820 --> 00:44:35,981
Up until I was 20 or 21, my entire career
was driven by pure love for the game.

672
00:44:36,090 --> 00:44:37,820
I couldn't wait to get out on the court.

673
00:44:37,918 --> 00:44:39,918
Especially on clay,
where I could construct points,

674
00:44:40,071 --> 00:44:42,431
think tactically, and really enjoy
every aspect of the game.

675
00:44:42,623 --> 00:44:47,553
And then everything changed. Suddenly,
it wasn't just a game anymore.

676
00:44:47,700 --> 00:44:49,748
Now it was my job.
People had expectations.

677
00:44:49,925 --> 00:44:51,842
You have to play this tournament...
You have to play that tournament...

678
00:44:51,991 --> 00:44:55,311
Reaching finals was no longer enough.
Now you were expected to win everything.

679
00:44:55,453 --> 00:44:59,240
Now you're world No. 1. Somehow,
the whole story changed.

680
00:44:59,559 --> 00:45:02,959
But you can't win tournaments
every single week.

681
00:45:03,260 --> 00:45:09,100
I didn't know how to deal with that.
How to be okay when I wasn't winning.

682
00:45:10,120 --> 00:45:15,046
That's when the spiral began...
The panic set in. -Yes, exactly.

683
00:45:15,455 --> 00:45:18,609
Is that what you started feeling?

684
00:45:19,012 --> 00:45:26,892
I felt like I had to live up to it because
I'd achieved the very highest level.

685
00:45:26,999 --> 00:45:29,374
And you start believing you
have to do it all the time.

686
00:45:29,489 --> 00:45:31,559
I have to keep doing it,
over and over again.

687
00:45:31,669 --> 00:45:33,616
And although there were
people around you...

688
00:45:33,700 --> 00:45:36,131
I'm sure they told you that you didn't
have to win every time? -Absolutely.

689
00:45:36,247 --> 00:45:41,012
It was something happening inside your
own mind, and no one could fix that.

690
00:45:41,192 --> 00:45:43,432
And then it became something
that no longer belonged to me.

691
00:45:43,562 --> 00:45:47,988
All of a sudden, it belonged
to everyone else. Their expectations.

692
00:45:48,254 --> 00:45:53,191
The media's expectations.
The sponsors. The fans...

693
00:45:53,421 --> 00:45:56,381
And suddenly, tennis
wasn't mine anymore.

694
00:45:56,496 --> 00:45:59,582
It was no longer my love or my game.
It was still my passion,

695
00:45:59,900 --> 00:46:05,020
but suddenly my game became
blocked by everything that came with it.

696
00:46:05,980 --> 00:46:08,780
And then the criticism starts to affect
you more... -Of course it does.

697
00:46:09,020 --> 00:46:14,860
It's getting unbearable. It's tearing you
apart. -Absolutely. -It destroys you.

698
00:46:15,053 --> 00:46:17,373
I'm a perfectionist.
I still am, even today.

699
00:46:17,541 --> 00:46:20,181
Whatever I do, I give it
one hundred percent.

700
00:46:20,319 --> 00:46:22,519
That's how I was when I trained, too.

701
00:46:22,719 --> 00:46:25,279
And then people started saying:
"She's lost interest in tennis."

702
00:46:25,479 --> 00:46:29,239
Meanwhile, I was pushing myself
even harder, training more than ever

703
00:46:29,477 --> 00:46:33,717
to get back into form. I was trying
absolutely everything...

704
00:46:33,980 --> 00:46:39,780
So hearing those things hurt even more.
You can't completely shut it out.

705
00:46:39,863 --> 00:46:41,863
Eventually, those comments reach you.

706
00:46:41,985 --> 00:46:44,785
Even though I never really read
articles about myself.

707
00:46:44,930 --> 00:46:48,090
Fortunately, social media
didn't exist back then. -Right.

708
00:46:48,236 --> 00:46:51,876
These days, it's much harder
for players. -One hundred percent.

709
00:46:52,711 --> 00:46:56,111
Let's move on for a moment,
we'll come back to that.

710
00:46:56,592 --> 00:46:59,752
It's a fascinating topic.

711
00:47:00,580 --> 00:47:07,414
If you were a tennis analyst, how would
you describe Ana Ivanović as a player?

712
00:47:07,559 --> 00:47:09,882
What would you say was
your biggest strength?

713
00:47:11,373 --> 00:47:13,555
You mentioned your forehand, right?

714
00:47:13,883 --> 00:47:17,625
Did you prefer long matches or short
ones? Long rallies or short rallies?

715
00:47:17,689 --> 00:47:19,976
Where did you feel most confident?

716
00:47:20,130 --> 00:47:22,890
"If I can get the point
into this game, I'll win it."

717
00:47:23,018 --> 00:47:24,811
And where did you feel vulnerable?

718
00:47:24,985 --> 00:47:26,945
When did you feel your opponent
had the advantage?

719
00:47:27,067 --> 00:47:30,123
Without a doubt, my forehand
was my biggest weapon.

720
00:47:30,423 --> 00:47:34,560
I always loved running around
my backhand to hit a forehand.

721
00:47:34,678 --> 00:47:36,998
When I could hit an inside-out forehand
from the backhand corner, I felt like

722
00:47:37,136 --> 00:47:39,976
the entire court belonged to me. Like
I could place the ball anywhere I wanted.

723
00:47:40,075 --> 00:47:43,576
So I was always trying to get my
opponents to play the ball there.

724
00:47:43,813 --> 00:47:47,177
I didn't like being pulled
wide on my forehand side,

725
00:47:47,494 --> 00:47:50,774
because it gave me fewer
angles to work with.

726
00:47:51,378 --> 00:47:55,271
When I won Roland Garros, that
was actually part of my game plan.

727
00:47:55,477 --> 00:47:58,717
I'd hit a slightly higher backhand
down the line,

728
00:47:58,918 --> 00:48:03,505
hoping my opponent would send back
a similar ball to my backhand,

729
00:48:03,613 --> 00:48:05,453
so I could run around it
and attack with my forehand.

730
00:48:05,543 --> 00:48:08,373
Or they'd play to my forehand directly,
and from there I could dictate the rally.

731
00:48:08,526 --> 00:48:13,060
So, in general, shorter rallies
were my strength.

732
00:48:13,263 --> 00:48:16,396
I definitely wasn't a fan
of very  long rallies.

733
00:48:16,682 --> 00:48:20,427
And here's another irony...
I played an aggressive style of tennis,

734
00:48:20,537 --> 00:48:25,740
yet I didn't enjoy facing players with strong
serves who finished points in one

735
00:48:25,940 --> 00:48:29,620
or two shots, because I never felt
I had the chance to get into the rally.

736
00:48:29,860 --> 00:48:33,940
As much as I disliked very long rallies,
I still wanted rhythm.

737
00:48:34,049 --> 00:48:36,964
I wanted the opportunity to construct
the point and execute a tactical plan.

738
00:48:37,059 --> 00:48:39,754
To create a situation where the opponent
gives you that slightly higher ball back.

739
00:48:39,853 --> 00:48:40,715
Exactly.

740
00:48:40,902 --> 00:48:45,848
I remember playing against Plíšková...
She had such a strong, awkward serve.

741
00:48:46,135 --> 00:48:50,730
I could never tell where
she was going to serve

742
00:48:50,869 --> 00:48:53,149
or what she was going to do
with the point.

743
00:48:54,100 --> 00:48:57,180
So she was your kryptonite?
-Yes, she definitely was.

744
00:48:57,380 --> 00:48:59,020
And whose kryptonite were you?

745
00:48:59,260 --> 00:49:02,420
I think... Svetlana Kuznetsova's
and Jelena Janković's.

746
00:49:03,100 --> 00:49:06,860
Who was your biggest rival?
Who did you consider your greatest rival?

747
00:49:07,060 --> 00:49:10,340
I'd say... Maria Sharapova, because
we belonged to the same generation.

748
00:49:10,500 --> 00:49:13,940
We played so many matches
against each other.

749
00:49:14,140 --> 00:49:16,500
And what about Jelena Janković?

750
00:49:16,940 --> 00:49:21,580
It's unusual for one country to have
two top players at the same time.

751
00:49:21,780 --> 00:49:26,260
That can create a great friendship,
but it can also create a unique rivalry.

752
00:49:26,700 --> 00:49:31,260
You're competing for the same fans,
after all. -Exactly.

753
00:49:31,460 --> 00:49:34,740
There aren't many close
friendships in tennis.

754
00:49:34,940 --> 00:49:39,860
Today, some of the girls I competed
against are among my closest friends.

755
00:49:40,140 --> 00:49:42,980
But back then, there really
wasn't much friendship.

756
00:49:43,140 --> 00:49:45,980
And I don't think it's only
the players' fault.

757
00:49:46,220 --> 00:49:49,380
It's also the whole team and the entire
environment around the sport.

758
00:49:49,580 --> 00:49:51,900
Coaches would tell you,
"You can't tell her about your problems.

759
00:49:52,100 --> 00:49:54,460
She might think you're struggling
and use that against you."

760
00:49:54,660 --> 00:49:58,980
So there's this sense of isolation,
especially in women's tennis.

761
00:49:59,180 --> 00:50:02,060
I think it's still like that today.

762
00:50:02,174 --> 00:50:07,334
The Spanish and French players
tend to stick together a bit more,

763
00:50:07,518 --> 00:50:12,522
and so do the Italians, but genuine
close friendships are rare.

764
00:50:14,060 --> 00:50:20,780
I know Goran and Novak have said
there aren't really friendships on tour,

765
00:50:21,240 --> 00:50:23,652
but they'll still go out to dinner with
other players during tournaments.

766
00:50:23,801 --> 00:50:25,870
That's not really the case
on the women's tour?

767
00:50:26,090 --> 00:50:30,170
Not really. It happens occasionally,
but generally, no.

768
00:50:30,276 --> 00:50:33,042
The men's tour has always been much
more relaxed in that respect.

